    "q-251038": {
      "dom_id": "step-q251038",
      "name": "Question 4",
      "problem": "Evaluate ${\\sum }_{a=1}^{3}a{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{a+1}=${{field-1}}",
      "worked_solution": "The sum takes integer values of $a$ from $1$ to $3.$ So, computing the terms for $a=1,2,3$ and then adding them, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{a=1}^{3}a{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{a+1} & =1{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{1+1}+2{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2+1}+3{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{3+1} \\\\  & =1{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+2{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{3}+3{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{4} \\\\  & =1\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}+2\\cdot \\frac{1}{8}+3\\cdot \\frac{1}{16} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}+\\frac{2}{8}+\\frac{3}{16} \\\\  & =\\frac{(1\\cdot 4)+(2\\cdot 2)+3}{16} \\\\  & =\\frac{4+4+3}{16} \\\\  & =\\frac{11}{16}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
